endobj

515 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 201 0 R/T(DISCUSSION)/E(DISCUSSION)/Pg 39 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

516 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 201 0 R/E(Feature Impact:)/Pg 39 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

517 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 201 0 R/E( everal variables show meaningful relationships with Falcon 9 landing outcomes. In particular, orbit type, payload mass, and flight number are strongly associated with success. For example, heavier payloads launched into LEO, SSO, and ISS orbits tend to have higher landing success rates, suggesting that certain mission profiles are more favorable for recovery.)/Pg 39 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

518 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 201 0 R/E(GTO Ambiguity:)/Pg 39 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

519 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 201 0 R/E( Geostationary Transfer Orbit \(GTO\) missions present a more complex pattern. Unlike other orbit types, GTO launches display mixed outcomes across a wide range of payload masses, with both successful and failed landings occurring. This overlap makes GTO cases harder to classify and increases prediction uncertainty for these missions.)/Pg 39 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

520 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 201 0 R/E(Why Machine Learning is Effective:)/Pg 39 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

521 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 201 0 R/E( No single feature is sufficient to fully explain landing success or failure. The outcome depends on interactions between multiple variables rather than isolated effects. Machine learning models are well-suited for this type of problem because they can capture non-linear relationships and complex feature interactions within historical launch data\204patterns that would be difficult to identify manually.)/Pg 39 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

522 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 201 0 R/E(Model Performance:)/Pg 39 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

523 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 201 0 R/E( Among the tested models, the Decision Tree achieved the best cross-validation performance. This suggests it is particularly effective at capturing non-linear decision boundaries in the dataset. In contrast, linear models such as Logistic Regression and SVM may struggle to fully represent these complex relationships, while KNN provides competitive but slightly less stable performance.)/Pg 39 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

524 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 201 0 R/Pg 39 0 R/A[648 0 R 0]/K[9]>>
endobj

525 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 202 0 R/Pg 40 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

526 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 202 0 R/E(-SpaceX Falcon 9 first-stage landing success can be effectively predicted using historical launch data, with the final model achieving approximately 83% accuracy on the test set. The objective of this project is to determine whether the first stage of a Falcon 9 rocket will successfully land after launch, since this outcome is directly tied to SpaceX\220s ability to reuse rockets and significantly reduce launch costs.)/Pg 40 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

527 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 202 0 R/E(-To address this problem, several machine learning models were developed and trained on past Falcon 9 mission data. These models learned relationships between key launch attributes such as payload mass, orbit type, launch site, and flight numberand the final landing outcome. Each of these features contributes differently to the prediction: for instance, certain orbit types and higher flight numbers are generally associated with higher success rates, while payload mass can influence the difficulty of recovery depending on mission requirements.)/Pg 40 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

528 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 202 0 R/E(-The exploratory analysis also highlights a strong evolution in SpaceX\220s landing performance over time. Early missions in 2010 had near-zero success rates, reflecting the experimental stage of reusable rocket technology. However, as SpaceX refined its booster recovery techniques, success rates increased steadily, reaching approximately 90% by 2019. This clear upward trend demonstrates both technological improvement and operational maturity.)/Pg 40 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

529 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 202 0 R/E(-Among the four machine learning models evaluated, the Decision Tree consistently achieved the best performance, with a cross-validation score of approximately 0.889. This suggests that it is better suited to capturing the non-linear relationships and interaction effects present in the dataset. Other models such as Logistic Regression and SVM, while stable, assume more linear decision boundaries and therefore may not fully capture the complexity of launch dynamics. K-Nearest Neighbors performed competitively but was slightly less robust compared to the Decision Tree.)/Pg 40 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

530 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 202 0 R/E(-Overall, the Decision Tree model provides the most reliable predictive capability for this problem. Its ability to model complex feature interactions makes it particularly effective for forecasting Falcon 9 landing outcomes. This predictive framework not only improves understanding of launch success factors but also offers a practical way to estimate SpaceX\220s effective launch costs based on the likelihood of first-stage reuse.)/Pg 40 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

